Fix BuffGetAnyLineDataW truncating any line that contains combining characters

BuffGetAnyLineDataW() has two counters that mean different things and are
initialised from the same value:

  copysize = min(NumOfColumns, bufsize - 1);   /* how many CELLS to walk   */
  ...
  left     = copysize;                          /* how much BUFFER is left */

`left` is passed straight to expand_wchar() as its `buf_size`, which counts
wchar_t UNITS, and is decremented by the number of units written. But a cell
holding a base character plus a combining mark writes two units, and a
surrogate pair writes two more. So on any line with combining characters the
unit budget is exhausted roughly twice as fast as the cell loop advances,
expand_wchar() reports `too_small`, and the loop breaks -- silently dropping
the rest of the line.

`left` should be seeded with the real size of the output buffer. The cell loop
is already bounded by `copysize`, so the two limits stay independent, which is
what the code reads as intending.

This is a SECOND defect in the same function as
0001-buffgetanylinedataw-padding.patch, with the same consequence for the same
sole caller (filesys_log.cpp:443): **session logging silently truncates lines**.
0001 truncates at the first full-width character; this one truncates any line
carrying combining marks, at about half the terminal width.

Reproduction, via the Sterna oracle (which runs vtterm.c + buffer.c headless).
Eight copies of "A U+3099 SPACE" into a 12-column terminal -- the cursor
arithmetic proves the buffer holds six of them on the first row:

    printf 'A\343\202\231 %.0s' $(seq 1 8) | oracle --cols 12 --rows 4

    before:  # cursor 4,1
             0 |A゙ A゙ A゙ A゙     |     <- four, and the row is not full
             1 |A゙ A゙         |

    after:   # cursor 4,1
             0 |A゙ A゙ A゙ A゙ A゙ A゙ |     <- six, filling all twelve columns
             1 |A゙ A゙         |

Found by diffing Tera Term's own tests/unicodebuf-combining1.sh against a
reimplementation. Tera Term 5.7.0-dev (commit 827a35b05).

